# TODO

- Revisit environment variables and multi-tenant / multi-project support
  Simplify the environment variable surface and clean up the current multi-tenant and multi-project model.

- Separate control plane and data plane concerns
  Establish a clear split between control plane and data plane responsibilities to prepare future features cleanly.

- Introduce a clear `rstream sandbox` distinction
  Evolve the package structure so `rstream tunnels` and `rstream sandbox` are distinct products under a shared `rstream` umbrella, instead of treating the current `rstream` package as effectively `rstream tunnels`.

- Keep managed and community / open-source deployments in scope
  Future changes for both `rstream tunnels` and `rstream sandbox` should work cleanly with managed deployments and with community / open-source deployments.

- Keep WebTTY E2E crypto agility scoped and explicit
  The protocol reserves ChaCha20-Poly1305 suite identifiers, but the current implementation intentionally supports only the AES-256-GCM payload suite and HPKE X25519 / HKDF-SHA256 / AES-256-GCM key envelopes. Do not enable ChaCha20-Poly1305 until all SDKs have audited implementations, shared test vectors, cross-SDK runtime coverage, and product documentation that explains suite negotiation and fail-closed behavior. In browsers, this also requires a careful WebCrypto support review before adding any non-native crypto dependency.

- Keep the WebTTY cybersecurity audit current
  The 2026-06-24 audit is stored outside the repository in the internal release reports. Update it before release when the WebTTY protocol, endpoint authentication, workspace-managed trust, recording encryption, log export, permissions, CLI/config/env surfaces, or cross-SDK runtime behavior change.

- Keep cross-SDK WebTTY mutual-auth coverage synchronized
  The JS WebTTY client can verify server identity and sign protocol-bound client proofs for E2E sessions. Keep the transcript format synchronized with the Go and C++ SDKs, and retain browser/runtime tests for explicit-key and workspace-managed trust providers before declaring the protocol stable.

- Upgrade the JavaScript build and release toolchain majors together
  TypeScript 7, ESLint 10, Changesets 3, and npm 12 need a coordinated compatibility and Node.js support review. The current public packages retain Node.js 18 support, while Changesets 3 requires Node.js 22 or newer and npm 12 requires newer Node.js 22 or 24 patch releases. Validate the ESLint and TypeScript plugin ecosystem and the immutable release workflow before raising these toolchain majors.
